    englishChapters: [
      [
        'At the close of the eleventh century CE, the political geography of eastern India was divided between the declining Somavamshi dynasty of Utkala-Kosala, the Pala rulers of Bengal, and the powerful Chola-Chalukya empire under Kulottunga I in Vengi and Tamil Nadu.',
        'Into this fluid balance stepped Anantavarman Chodaganga Deva, who transformed the regional Eastern Ganga principality of Kalinganagara into a pan-eastern Indian imperial state.'
      ],
      [
        'Multiple copper-plate charters—including the Korni (1112 CE) and Vizagapatam grants—record his exact parentage: his father was Eastern Ganga king Rajaraja I Devendravarman and his mother was Rajasundari, daughter of Chola Emperor Virarajendra (making Chodaganga the maternal grandson of the Chola imperial house).',
        'Crowned at a very young age on 17 February 1078 CE at Kalinganagara (modern Mukhalingam, Srikakulam district, Andhra Pradesh), he ruled for an extraordinary 69 years until 1147 CE.'
      ],
      [
        'Chodaganga Deva systematically integrated the three historic regions of Trikalinga—Kalinga, Utkala, and Odra—extending imperial borders from the Hooghly-Ganga river in southern Bengal (Mandara/Aramgarh) down to the Godavari delta.',
        'Around 1135 CE, recognizing the strategic centrality of the Mahanadi delta, he moved the administrative capital northward from Mukhalingam to Sarangagarh and Abhinava Varanasi Kataka (modern Cuttack), which remained Odisha’s royal capital for the next four centuries.'
      ],
      [
        'Early in his reign, Chodaganga successfully withstood two Chola invasions of Kalinga (c. 1096 and 1110 CE, celebrated in Jayankondar’s Tamil war poem Kalingattupparani) and rebuilt Kalinga’s military strength through fortified riverine citadels and maritime trade revenues.',
        'His diplomacy balanced deference to senior temples with firm assertion of Trikalingadhipati sovereignty.'
      ],
      [
        'Between 1110 and 1112 CE, Chodaganga Deva defeated the last Somavamshi king Karnadeva and annexed Utkala (coastal Odisha), uniting north and south Odisha under a single crown for the first time in centuries. Subsequently, he expanded northward into Dandabhukti and Suhmas (southwestern Bengal) and campaigned along the Godavari against Vengi rivals.',
        'This Ganga-to-Godavari territorial framework established the enduring political footprint inherited three centuries later by Kapilendra Deva Gajapathi.'
      ],
      [
        'His long reign intersected with remarkable figures across India: Chola Emperor Kulottunga I (his maternal uncle and rival), Somavamshi King Karnadeva, Kalachuri Ratnadeva II of Ratanpur, and the great Vaishnava philosopher Sri Ramanujacharya, whose pilgrimage to Puri and Srikurmam occurred during this era.',
        'Inscriptions at Draksharama, Simhachalam, Mukhalingam, and Puri record the generous temple endowments of his queens (Kasturikamodini, Indira, Chandralekha) and military officers.'
      ],
      [
        'Chodaganga Deva’s supreme cultural monument is the existing grand Sri Jagannatha Temple at Puri, whose construction he initiated around 1135–1136 CE (later completed by his grandson Anangabhima Deva III), as explicitly attested by the 1198 CE Dasgoba Copper Plates of Rajaraja III.',
        'He equally patronized Srikurmam (Srikakulam), Simhachalam (Visakhapatnam), Mukhalingam, and Draksharama, creating an integrated sacred architectural corridor linking coastal Andhra and Odisha.'
      ],
      [
        'His career is anchored in over 100 stone and copper-plate inscriptions across Andhra Pradesh and Odisha: the Korni Copper Plates (1081 & 1112 CE), Vizagapatam Plates (1081, 1118, 1135 CE), Lingaraja and Mukhalingam stone records, Draksharama epigraphs, and the Dasgoba Plates (1198 CE).',
        'These contemporary epigraphs provide exact Shaka Samvat dates for his coronation (17 February 1078 CE), Utkala annexation, religious titles, and Puri temple foundation.'
      ],
      [
        'One of the most illuminating scholarly discoveries from Chodaganga’s own inscriptions is his religious synthesis: in 1112 CE (Korni Plates) he bears both Parama-Mahesvara (Shaiva) and Parama-Vaishnava titles, whereas by 1118 CE (Vizagapatam Plates) he emphasizes Parama-Vaishnava devotion alongside continued Shaiva patronage.',
        'Historians interpret this not as sectarian rejection but as inclusive statecraft uniting Shaiva Kalinga-Utkala and Vaishnava Jagannatha traditions.'
      ],
      [
        'By establishing Cuttack as capital and raising the monumental Puri Jagannatha temple, Anantavarman Chodaganga Deva created the political and sacred heart of Odisha while maintaining deep historical roots in north coastal Andhra.',
        'His achievements directly link Royal Sovereignty (CH-0024), Sacred Temple Architecture (CH-0021), Shilpi Craftsmanship (CH-0020), and Fortified Capital Engineering (CH-0019).'
      ]
    ],
