    englishChapters: [
      [
        'When Emperor Kapilendra Deva passed away near the Krishna river in late 1466 CE, he bypassed his eldest warrior-son Kumara Hamvira and designated his younger son Purushottama Deva as successor. This unexpected nomination sparked a decade of civil conflict (1466–1476 CE) during which the Bahmani Sultanate seized Kondavidu and Rajahmundry while Saluva Narasimha of Vijayanagara took Udayagiri.',
        'Purushottama Deva’s resilience in overcoming this severe geopolitical crisis defined his thirty-year reign (1467–1497 CE).'
      ],
      [
        'Contemporary inscriptions—including the 1489 CE Potavaram Copper-Plate Grant and Puri Jagannatha edicts—confirm Purushottama Deva as the son and royal successor of Kapilendra Deva in the Suryavamshi Gajapathi line. His chief queen was Rupambika, and his son Prataparudra Deva inherited the imperial throne in 1497 CE.',
        'Later Odia literary traditions celebrate his legendary marriage to Princess Padmavati of Kanchi, though epigraphy identifies his documented queen as Rupambika.'
      ],
      [
        'Purushottama’s strategic priority was recovering the fertile deltaic provinces and hill fortresses of coastal Andhra—Rajahmundry on the Godavari, Kondavidu in Guntur, and Udayagiri in Nellore—which formed the economic and military buffer protecting Odisha from southern and Deccan powers.',
        'By 1490–1491 CE, Gajapathi banners flew once more from the Hooghly-Ganga frontier down to the Pennar river in Nellore.'
      ],
      [
        'After rallying loyal Odra feudatories to recapture Cuttack from Hamvira’s Bahmani-backed coalition around 1476 CE, Purushottama focused on domestic reconciliation, fiscal recovery, and strengthening Jagannatha temple institutions at Puri.',
        'His Jagannatha Temple inscriptions record strict protection of temple endowments and affirmation of Brahmin and community land grants.'
      ],
      [
        'Capitalizing on the post-1481 fragmentation of the Bahmani Sultanate following Sultan Muhammad Shah III’s death, Purushottama Deva launched decisive southern campaigns in 1484–1485 CE, expelling Bahmani garrisons from Rajahmundry and Kondavidu.',
        'Between 1489 and 1491 CE, he defeated Vijayanagara’s Saluva Narasimha near Udayagiri (confirmed by the 1489 Potavaram Grant and Sarasvativilasam), recovering Udayagiri fortress and re-establishing Suryavamshi Gajapathi supremacy down to Nellore.'
      ],
      [
        'Purushottama Deva’s court attracted distinguished Sanskrit scholars, poets, and jurists, including Kavichandra Ray Divakara Mishra. Externally, he navigated rivalry with Bahmani Minister Mahmud Gawan, Sultan Shihab-ud-din Mahmud, and Vijayanagara’s Saluva Narasimha—achieving a stable southern equilibrium by the 1490s.',
        'His diplomacy ensured that Prataparudra Deva inherited an intact and prosperous empire in 1497 CE.'
      ],
      [
        'Purushottama Deva was one of medieval India’s foremost scholar-monarchs. He authored or commissioned the Abhinava Gitagovinda, Mukti-Chintamani, Nama-Malika, Abhinava Venisamhara, and Gopalarchana-Vidhi, blending Vaishnava devotion with Sanskrit literary polish.',
        'Architecturally, he constructed the ornate Bhoga-Mandapa (Hall of Offerings) of the Puri Sri Jagannatha Temple and installed the celebrated Sakshi-Gopala and Bhanda-Ganesha shrines revered in Odia cultural memory.'
      ],
      [
        'Key primary sources verifying Purushottama Deva’s reign include the Potavaram Copper-Plate Grant (1489 CE, Prakasam/Nellore district), Jagannatha Temple Puri Odia inscriptions (1467–1496 CE), Kondavidu and Simhachalam stone records, and the Sanskrit preface of Prataparudra’s Sarasvativilasam.',
        'These records independently confirm his exact Anka regnal years, reconquest of Kondavidu and Udayagiri, and literary works.'
      ],
      [
        'Critical historiography carefully evaluates the famous Kanchi-Kaveri tradition (immortalized later by Purushottama Dasa’s Kanchi-Kaveri ballad, wherein Lord Jagannatha and Balabhadra ride as white and black horsemen to aid the king against Kanchi).',
        'Historians note that while Gajapathi forces did defeat Saluva Narasimha (who ruled Chandragiri-Kanchi) and brought back sacred icons (Sakshi-Gopala and Ganesha), the miraculous divine horsemen motif and the Padmavati romance belong to devotional literature rather than contemporary epigraphy.'
      ],
      [
        'Emperor Purushottama Deva stabilized the Suryavamshi Gajapathi Empire after its gravest internal crisis, bequeathed enduring Sanskrit scholarship, and deeply embedded the Chhera Pahanra (ceremonial sweeping of the Jagannatha chariot) in Odisha’s cultural soul.',
        'His reign connects directly to Royal Heritage (CH-0024), Martial Recovery (CH-0017), Sacred Temple Architecture (CH-0021), and Scholarly Patronage (CH-0025).'
      ]
    ],
